Do not send the current packet.

The actual object is a usable project name, and the acting subject is the human Principal. The field’s history produced unclassified literary artifacts under two generation treatments; a later projection—not the field—selected items that resemble names. Therefore, the proposed packet compares treatment-plus-projection surfaces, not naming methods.

The organizing contradiction is that the project needs a practical naming decision, while the packet is structured as an experiment about memory versus retrieval. Retrieval changed cost and source participation, but there is no evidence that it improved aesthetic quality or name usability. Both treatments converged on similar language, and the missing direct-naming baseline prevents the Principal from knowing whether either archive offers value over simply asking for names.

Next action: add a small, plain direct-generation naming arm using the same project brief, then create one provenance-blinded shortlist across all three sources. Apply the same name-likeness and eligibility criteria to every candidate. Include “none of these” or “request another round.”

Evidence limits:

- The packet can support a Principal’s preference among presented candidates.
- It cannot establish that retrieval is aesthetically superior.
- It cannot attribute preferences cleanly to generation treatment because projection intervened.
- It cannot show that the generative field outperforms direct naming without a baseline.
- Higher token cost and broader source participation are process findings, not evidence of better names.

Decision boundary: send only when the Principal can choose among blinded, usable candidates that include the direct baseline and can reject the set. If the immediate decision is strictly “choose one of these already-vetted names,” the existing items may be reused—but not as the current two-arm treatment comparison.